ntdll: map PE images into anonymous memory on Android.

Wine could load ntdll.so and answer `wine --version` inside the app sandbox,
but building a prefix died with:

    err:virtual:map_image_into_view failed to set 60000020 protection on
    L"\\??\\C:\\windows\\system32\\ntdll.dll" section .text, noexec filesystem?
    err:virtual:... section .hexpthk, noexec filesystem?
    err:virtual:virtual_setup_exception stack overflow 416 bytes addr 0x6fffea6850

The last line is the consequence, not a second bug: execution reached ntdll's
.text, which had never been made executable, and faulted.

Upstream's "noexec filesystem?" is a guess, and on Android it is the wrong one.
Measured on the device (Motorola Signature, Android 16, SM8845), all three
obvious explanations are false:

  - SELinux logs `granted { execute }` on app_data_file for this process.
  - /data/user/0 is mounted rw, not noexec.
  - Page size is 4096, so PE sections are not sub-page.

tools/probe/mapexec.c narrows it to one line. Against the very same ntdll.dll,
in the app's own uid:

    ok    mmap R  /  then mprotect RX
    ok    mmap RX directly
    ok    MAP_FIXED RX over a PROT_NONE reservation
    ok    clean private page -> RX
    FAIL  DIRTIED private page -> RX (execmod): Permission denied (errno 13)

Writing one byte to a private file mapping is the whole difference. Making a
*modified* file-backed mapping executable requires the SELinux execmod
permission, which apps do not have; parts of the policy dontaudit it, which is
why the denial never appears in logcat.

Wine writes relocations into an image before protecting its sections, so any PE
loaded away from its ImageBase dirties its own .text and can then never execute.
That is exactly the observed split: wineboot.exe landed at its preferred base
0x140000000, was never written to, and mapped `c-r-x` fine; ntdll.dll was
relocated to 0x6fffe40000 and failed.

Wine already has the code path needed -- when `removable` is set,
map_file_into_view skips mmap and pread()s each section into the view's
anonymous memory, where PROT_EXEC needs only execmem, which every JIT on the
platform relies on. This forces that path for image mappings on Android.

The cost is real: image pages become private copies instead of shared,
demand-paged file pages, so each process pays full RSS for its DLLs and they are
not shared between processes. There is no cheaper option -- any scheme that
keeps the pages file-backed runs into execmod the moment a relocation is applied.

Not suitable upstream as-is: a real fix would gate on a configure check for the
platform's willingness to execute modified file mappings rather than on
__ANDROID__, and would ideally narrow the fallback to images that actually get
relocated.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index 6e1697720f..9689781320 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -3282,6 +3282,26 @@ static NTSTATUS map_image_into_view( struct file_view *view, const UNICODE_STRIN
     SIZE_T align_mask = max( image_info->alignment - 1, page_mask );
     INT_PTR delta;
 
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+    /* Never execute from file-backed pages on Android.
+     *
+     * A PE loaded away from its ImageBase has relocations written into it
+     * before its sections are protected. Those writes dirty COW pages of the
+     * private file mapping, and making a *modified* file mapping executable is
+     * a distinct SELinux permission -- execmod -- which apps are not granted.
+     * The result is an mprotect that fails with EACCES, no audit line (parts
+     * of the policy dontaudit execmod), and a crash the moment anything calls
+     * into the section.
+     *
+     * Forcing the removable path makes map_file_into_view pread() each section
+     * into the view's anonymous memory instead, where PROT_EXEC needs only
+     * execmem -- which apps do have, because every JIT on the platform needs
+     * it. The cost is that image pages become private copies rather than
+     * shared, demand-paged file pages.
+     */
+    removable = TRUE;
+#endif
+
     TRACE_(module)( "mapping PE file %s at %p-%p\n", debugstr_us(nt_name), ptr, ptr + total_size );
 
     /* map the header */
